<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cacamatzin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1483–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cacamatzin was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> from 1516 to 1520, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualpilli">Nezahualpilli</a> and nephew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. Chosen with Mexica backing over his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ixtlilxochitl-ii">Ixtlilxochitl II</a>, he was seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1520 after plotting resistance to the Spanish. He was killed in Spanish custody in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in June 1520.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cacamatzin.jpg" alt="Cacamatzin" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuscript by Fray Bernardino de Sahagún. Illustration by an unknown artist. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacamatzin,_~1494_-_7-1520,_retrato_an%C3%B3nimo_(siglo_XVI).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cacamatzin, also called Cacama, was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualpilli">Nezahualpilli</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> by one of his mistresses. Texcoco was the second most important city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, and its kings were traditionally elected by the nobility from the ablest members of the royal family. His election was said to have been made under heavy pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, who wished to lessen Texcoco's power in favour of greater centralisation in his own city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Spaniards, lodged in the palace of Axayácatl, found the treasure of Moctezuma's grandfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> demanded that Moctezuma's vassals pay all the gold they had. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> the task fell to two of Cacamatzin's brothers, Tetlahuehuezquititzin and Netzahualquentzin. Suspected of treason after a misunderstanding, Netzahualquentzin was sentenced by Cortés to hang; Moctezuma interceded and the sentence was suspended, but he was flogged.</p><p>Angered by these events, Cacamatzin called on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and neighbouring rulers to rise against the Spanish: his cousin the lord of Coyoacán, Totoquihuatzin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>, Cuitláhuac of Iztapalapa and the lord of Matlatzinco. Moctezuma, who did not want war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, warned Cortés, who tried to make peace. Cacamatzin refused, saying he would not be persuaded as Moctezuma had been. He gave an ultimatum of four days, urged his uncle to be a lord and not a slave, and declared that his war was for his vassals and the defence of their land and religion.</p><p>On the orders of Cortés, Moctezuma had Cacamatzin arrested in his own palace while he was discussing preparations for war; the lords of Coyoacán, Iztapalapa and Tacuba were also seized. Cacamatzin is credited with a poem, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Cacamatzin, which invokes his father and grandfather and seems to protest against the attack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> during the festival of Tóxcatl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cacamatzin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last independent king <b>Cacamatzin</b> died in Spanish custody in 1520.</blockquote><a href="/state/texcoco">Texcoco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1507" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Cacamatzin"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4267 5869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4267 5869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1507">What was here in 1507? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
